Here is an example of how you can implement session management using Google OAuth in a React application using classes instead of effects and Material Design:
```
// Import necessary libraries and components
import React, { Component } from 'react';
import { GoogleLogin } from 'react-google-login';
import { Grid, Card, CardContent } from '@material-ui/core';

// Define the Google OAuth API client ID and authorized react app URL
const clientId = 'YOUR_CLIENT_ID';
const authorizedUrl = 'http://localhost:3000/authorized';

// Define the session storage
class SessionManager extends Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state = {
      isLoggedIn: false,
      sessionToken: null
    };
  }

  // Handle Google OAuth login
  handleGoogleLogin = (response) => {
    const { clientId, code } = response;
    // Make a POST request to the Google OAuth token endpoint to exchange the code for an access token
    fetch(`https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/token?grant_type=authorization_code&code=${code}&redirect_uri=${authorizedUrl}&client_id=${clientId}&client_secret=YOUR_CLIENT_SECRET`)
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(data => {
        // Store the access token in session storage
        sessionStorage.setItem('sessionToken', data.access_token);
        this.setState({ isLoggedIn: true, sessionToken: data.access_token });
      })
      .catch(error => console.error(error));
  };

  // Handle Google OAuth logout
  handleGoogleLogout = () => {
    // Remove the access token from session storage
    sessionStorage.removeItem('sessionToken');
    this.setState({ isLoggedIn: false, sessionToken: null });
  };

  render() {
    return (
      <Grid container spacing={2}>
        <Grid item xs={12}>
          <Card>
            <CardContent>
              {this.state.isLoggedIn ? (
                <p>You are logged in!</p>
              ) : (
                <GoogleLogin
                  clientId={clientId}
                  onSuccess={this.handleGoogleLogin}
                  onFailure={console.error}
                  scope="profile email"
                >
                  Log in with Google
                </GoogleLogin>
              )}
            </CardContent>
          </Card>
        </Grid>
      </Grid>
    );
  }
}

export default SessionManager;
```
In this example, we define a `SessionManager` class that handles the session storage and Google OAuth login and logout logic. We use the `sessionStorage` API to store the access token.

The `handleGoogleLogin` method is called when the user grants access to the app, and it makes a POST request to the Google OAuth token endpoint to exchange the authorization code for an access token. We then store the access token in session storage and update the component state to indicate that the user is logged in.

The `handleGoogleLogout` method is called when the user clicks the logout button, and it removes the access token from session storage and updates the component state to indicate that the user is no longer logged in.

In the `render` method, we use a conditional statement to determine whether to display the login or logout button based on the component state. If the user is logged in, we display a message indicating that they are logged in. If they are not logged in, we display the Google OAuth login button.

